<GovernanceLayout
  title="How CRI Works"
  description="How Commercial Roofing Intel operates: how content is produced, how the business sustains itself, and how the two are kept separate."
  crumbName="How CRI Works"
  lede="A plain-language explanation of how this platform operates — how content gets made, how the business earns money, and the wall between the two."
  updated="2026-07-15"
>
  <h2>1. We publish knowledge</h2>
  <p>
    CRI produces encyclopedia references, decision guides, comparisons, situation guidance,
    decision tools, and datasets about commercial roofing. Everything is produced under our
    <a href="/about/editorial-standards/">Editorial Standards</a> and, for anything with
    numbers, our <a href="/about/methodology/">Methodology</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>2. You use it freely</h2>
  <p>
    The knowledge base is free. No paywall, no required registration, no gating of safety
    or decision-critical information. Most readers use CRI, make better decisions, and we
    never hear from them. That is the platform working as designed.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Some readers ask to be connected</h2>
  <p>
    Some readers conclude they need professional roofing services. If a reader asks, CRI
    can refer the opportunity to a vetted commercial roofing contractor. Referrals happen
    only at the reader’s request — educational pages do not push services.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Referrals fund the platform</h2>
  <p>
    Contractors may pay CRI for qualified referrals. That revenue funds the research,
    writing, review, tools, and data work you see here. Every relationship is disclosed on
    <a href="/about/funding-and-relationships/">Funding &amp; Relationships</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>The wall between money and editorial</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Editorial decisions are made without referral-partner input, review, or veto.</li>
    <li>No page exists to generate referrals; pages exist to answer decisions.</li>
    <li>Referral options are always labeled and always separate from editorial content.</li>
    <li>If we ever recommend against action that would have produced a referral, the reader’s interest wins. That is the test.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="vetting">How CRI content is vetted</h2>
  <p>Every piece of CRI content must:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>Be based on reputable industry sources, applicable building codes, manufacturer documentation, engineering guidance, and field experience where appropriate.</li>
    <li>Distinguish clearly between verified facts, industry best practices, and editorial analysis.</li>
    <li>Be technically reviewed before publication when construction methods or repair recommendations are discussed.</li>
    <li>Be updated when significant standards, regulations, or industry guidance change.</li>
    <li>Disclose any relevant conflicts of interest or business relationships.</li>
    <li>Include citations for factual claims, statistics, and technical specifications whenever practical.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="partner-standards">CRI Referral Partner Standards</h2>
  <p>
    "Vetted" is a promise, so here is its definition. A contractor may receive CRI
    referrals only while meeting every criterion below:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>Valid state commercial roofing license.</li>
    <li>Current general liability and workers' compensation insurance.</li>
    <li>Verifiable client references from completed commercial projects.</li>
    <li>Manufacturer certifications for the systems they install.</li>
    <li>A written agreement prohibiting high-pressure sales tactics.</li>
    <li>Agreement that unresolved client disputes are grounds for removal from the CRI network.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    These standards apply to every partner, including partners that share ownership with
    CRI — see <a href="/about/funding-and-relationships/">Funding &amp; Relationships</a>.
    If your experience with a referred contractor falls short of them,
    <a href="/contact/">report it</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Why this model</h2>
  <p>
    Advertising-funded publishing optimizes for attention. Contractor-owned content
    optimizes for sales. A referral model funded by disclosed, reader-initiated
    connections lets CRI optimize for the only thing that compounds: being right and
    being trusted.
  </p>
</GovernanceLayout>
